Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are porous and crystalline structures consisting of organic ligands are arranged between metal-ion nodes [13, 14]. MOFs have drawn great attention from the academy and industry in the past three decades because of their unique features, such as high surface area, steerable porous structure, and robust chemical tunability [15, 16]. Especially, MOFs are highly attractive for gas storage or separation [17], catalyzes [18], photo-communication [19, 20], optical filter [21, 22], and sensing [23, 24]. Reports on PC sensors based on MOFs have been fabricated by integrating functional building blocks of MOFs and dielectric nanoparticles that can generate refractive index-dependent optical signals to analytes for sensing [25–27]. However, vacancies and structural defects form between the MOFs and the dielectric layers and/or inside the stacking layers own to the lattice constant mismatch of these different materials and their stacking method. MOF-on-MOF structure allows
sequential growth of different types of MOFs with different lattice constants to sieve or filter molecules and reduce cross-talk in MOF-based  sensorics  [28–30].  However,  the  multilayered  MOFs systems lead to increased incoherent interference with light and reduced tuning ability due to the RI indifference of MOFs and the elements such as dangling bonds at the interface acting as trap centers,  complex  centers  and  participating  in  nonradiative complexes [31–33]. Thus, the challenge of MOFs-based PCs sensor lies in the strong incoherent scattering due to the mismatch of lattice constants between refractive layers [34–36].
Here we show the design of MOFs-based PCs with latticematched  isostructural  MOF  thin  films  by  a  layer-by-layer assembly method, which demonstrates tunable structural colors, improved  reflectivity,  and  enhanced  detection  sensitivity  to chemicals. Ethanolic solutions of different types of NPs, such as \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2},\mathrm{ZrO}_{2},\) and \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) were applied to modulate the RI of HKUST1  thin  films  (HKUST-1@NPs)  without  changing  the  lattice parameters, porosities, and optical adsorption in visible light. The isostructural MOFs-based PCs were constructed by sequentially spraying coating low RI layer of HKUST-1 and high RI layer of HKUST-1@NPs to form the desired number of Bragg bilayers, which homogenously encapsulated NPs in certain domains of the multilayered HKUST-1 films. Bandwidth of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@NPs PCs was tailored by the thickness and RI of HKUST-1 and HKUST-1@NPs layers, which demonstrate wide-range color tunable reflection. The isostructural MOFs-based PCs demonstrated excellent reflectivity of over \(96\%\) . In addition,
we achieved high detection sensitivity of chemicals on the isostructural MOFs-based PCs. Dynamic characteristics of sensing on the different types of MOFs-based PCs were demonstrated by characterizing the evolution of time-dependent reflectance. The results showed that the specific adsorption process of isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@NPs PCs could be tailored for chemical sensing by hydrogen bonding interactions between \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups on the surface of NPs and chemicals.
## 2 Results and discussion
Figure 1(a) depicts the construction of isostructural MOF-based PCs on a silicon wafer by stacking Bragg bilayers with spaced low and high RI of MOF thin films, in which the high RI layers of MOFs were tuned by selectively encapsulating high refractive index metal oxide NPs. Liquid phase epitaxy (LPE) [37, 38] method was used to fabricate monolithic MOF thin films in a layer-by-layer fashion. In brief, ethanolic solution of copper acetate \((\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2})\) and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\right)\) were sprayed in a sequential fashion to grow HKUST-1 thin films on fused silica substrates. By increasing the spraying cycles, the thickness of HKUST-1 varied from 40 to \(150\ \mathrm{nm}\) along with the increase of spraying cycles (Fig. 1(b), top, in black), which showed a linear increase with a slope of 2.6. The RI of HKUST-1 measured by a spectroscopic ellipsometer was ranging from 1.40 to 1.50 with the increasing spraying cycles (Fig. 1(b), bottom, in black). Then, we optimized the LPE method to modulate the refractive index of MOFs thin film by encapsulating colloidal NPs in the matrix [39],
