where \(m\) is the reflection order; \(n\) represents the average refractive index of the period (HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\)); and \(d\) represents the total thickness of the period. We calculated the reflection band of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) PCs with the thickness changing from 140 to \(240\;\mathrm{nm}\), corresponding to the average refractive index of 1.36–1.52 (Table S1 in the ESM). The reflection band of the isostructural MOFs-based PCs is regulated by the spraying time of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NPs solution to control their cumulative content. Thus, we designed the PCs with reflection bands tunable in the visible frequency at \(\lambda=415\), 450, 506, 556, 598, 629, and \(683\;\mathrm{nm}\), accompanied by thickness variations from 435–660 nm (Fig. S5 in the ESM).
Numerical modeling using transfer matrix methods was employed to describe the influence of refractive index contrast on reflectance (Fig. 1(c)). The simulated results (dash line) agree with the experimental results (solid line) of the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) PCs. Notably, the simulated results show multiple levels of interference peaks, while only one main peak appears in our experiment. The reason is that the finite difference time domain (FDTD) simulation cannot express the matching effect at the interface, which increases the chance of non-coherent interference [43]. So, the optical purity of the PCs is contributed to the design of the isostructural HKUST-1/ HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) stacking without lattice mismatch between Bragg bilayers. Consistently, the optical photography showed variable coloration from blue to red on the PCs (Fig. 1(d)). Converting the reflection spectra of the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) PCs to CIE 1931 chromaticity coordinates indicates good color tunability (Fig. 1(e)). Thus, the reflection band of the isostructural MOFs-based PCs can be regulated via selective encapsulation of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NPs in spaced layer of HKUST-1
films to tune optical constants of the target domains over broad ranges.
Figure 2(a) shows the representative cross-sectional images of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) PCs with different number of Bragg bilayers. We observed monolithic films of the PCs with thickness of 190, 390, and 580 nm corresponding to the stacking number of bilayers from \(N=1\) to 3. Notably, reflected color of the PCs with different Bragg bilayers \((N=1,2,\) and 3) was changing from blue-green to grass green and green, respectively (Fig. 2(a), inset). We further exploited reflection of the engineered isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) PCs at variable Bragg bilayers (Fig. 2(b)). The reflectivity of one Bragg bilayer \((N=1)\) was about 57.54% at \(\lambda=505\;\mathrm{nm}\), which gradually increased to 96.48% at \(N=3\). Also, full-width-at-half-maximum (FWHM) of reflection band of the PCs narrowed from 300 to 200 nm. In contrast, the reported MOFs-based PCs [44–47] were alternatingly stacked with nanoparticles of MOFs and the high RI materials with a clear multilayer interface by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) cross-section analysis, which led to more light scattering and decreased reflectivity of the PCs.
To confirm the multilayered structure of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) PCs, we conducted XPS depth profiling to resolve the material distribution profile perpendicular to the substrate (Figs. 2(c) and 2(d)). The energy-filtered transmission electron microscopy (EFTEM) technique is less quantitative for thin film analysis of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) in the high RI layer (HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\)) of the isostructural HKUST-1-based PCs due to their small concentrations across the domains. Instead, XPS depth profiling provides an alternative way to probe domain profiles in well-ordered nanostructures of the 1D PCs [48]. Figure 2(c) shows the alternating C 1s, Cu 2p, Si 2p, and Ti 2p signals of the isostructural MOFs-based PCs depending on the sputtering time. For the analysis, a diameter of \(200\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) region was continuously analyzed using \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\) ion source, while a concentric area of \(600\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\times1200\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) was eroded with a \(15\;\mathrm{kV}\) Ar sputter beam up to a final pass energy of \(112\;\mathrm{eV}\). The Cu 2P signals characterized the metal-cluster of the HKUST-1 layers, which rose rapidly at the beginning of sputtering and decreased slowly to zero after a long period of stabilization. A significant decrease of the C 1s signal was identified near the silicon substrate along with an increase of the Si 2p signal from the Si substrate. Ti 2P signals characterized the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NPs of the HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) films, which indicated the appearance of the high RI layers of the PCs. We zoomed in the alternating depth distribution of the Ti 2p spectra (Fig. 2(d)). Notably, periodic distribution of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) signals fluctuation was clearly observed along with the sputtering time, in which the yellow shade represented the HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) layers and the blue shade corresponded to the HKUST-1 layers. Thus, XPS depth profiling proved periodic high and low RI layers of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) PCs.
Visual detection and sensitive discrimination of chemicals is a critical characteristic for the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) PCs. We introduced chemical vapor of analytes on the PCs to monitor the real-time change of optical reflection path by ultraviolet–visible spectrophotometry (UV–vis). Figure 3(a) shows the reflection spectra of the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\) PCs (70 nm HKUST-1/90 nm HKUST-1@TiO\(_{2}\)) with blue-green color during the ethanol (EtOH) sensing process. The primary reflection band (at \(\lambda=465\;\mathrm{nm}\)) experienced a red-shift to 535 nm within 5 s when exposed to ethanol vapor, and the large wavelength shift (70 nm) demonstrated high sensitivity of the PCs. The corresponding color coordinates were presented from green to yellow, which was directly distinguishable by the naked eye (inset of Fig. 3(a)). Also, we conducted acetone (ACE), hexane (Hex), isopropanol (IPA), trichloromethane (TCH), methanol (MeOH),
dichloromethane (DCM), xylene (XYL), and toluene (TOL) sensing on the PCs, which showed a significant spectral red-shift of 16, 17, 37, 41, 45, 50, 52, and \(55~\mathrm{nm}\) , respectively (Fig. S6(a) in the ESM). The values of wavelength shift \((\Delta\lambda)\) and reflectance shift \((\Delta R)\) at the maximum reflectance were summarized to demonstrate a clear distinction for sensing these chemicals (Fig. S6(b) in the ESM). We performed principal component analysis (PCA) to the multivariate calibration of the characteristic chemical sensing of the PCs. A two-dimensional (2D) projection of the factor scores was calculated for each analyte (Fig. 3(b)), whereas the principal component first (PC1) and the principal component second (PC2) account for the most significant variance of the measurements. Firstly, the parameters \((\Delta\lambda,\ \ \Delta R,\ \ \Delta\mathrm{RI})\) were extracted from reflection spectra to analyze the individual capability of the PCs to discriminate between different analyte vapors (Table S2 in the ESM). Then, the PC1 and PC2 were calculated to give the \(x-\) and \(y.\) -axis of the PCA plot (Table S3 in the ESM), which accounted for \(93.2\%\) of the variance (Fig. 3(b)). We found a clear separation of all nine chemicals on the PCs, which indicated high sensitivity for discrimination of the analytes. Interestingly, sensing of the xylene had a strong dependence on PC2, while sensing of ethanol had great dependence on PC1. Report of HKUST-1 has specific adsorption for benzene molecules due to the host–guest interactions, while the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) encapsulated HKUST-1 film \((\mathrm{HKUST-1@TiO}_{2})\) has selective adsorption for ethanol molecules due to hydrogen bonding interactions [39]. Based on the consistency of the above findings with our PCA patterns, we can equate PC1 to the weighted effect of HKUST\(1\ @\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and PC2 to the weighted effect of HKUST-1 for chemical sensing. Thus, the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO2 PCs discriminate between the investigated analytes and achieve specific adsorption on ethanol due to the existence of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NPs in HKUST-1. To investigate the stability and repeatability of
chemical sensing on the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO2 PCs, we tested the reversible reflection band shifts upon alternately exposing \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and different chemicals for analysis (Fig. 3(c) and Fig. S7 in the ESM). Quick response without showing significant variations of reflectance \((\pm0.5\%\) in \(\mathrm{R}\%\) ) was obtained for 20 cycles repetition of the sensing process, suggesting that the resonators possessed excellent stability for sensing.
To evaluate the specific chemical sensing on the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO2 PCs, we conducted dynamic sensing analysis by characterizing concentration dependent spectrum-shift of the different chemicals (Fig. 3(d) and Figs. S8 and S9 in the ESM). The main reflection band of the HKUST-1/HKUST\(1\ @\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) PCs shifted from 465 to \(536~\mathrm{nm}\) by gradually increasing ethanol concentration to \(500\;\mathrm{ppm}\) , which showed a linear increase with a slope of 0.15. We found that detection limit of the reflectance was \(7.1\,\mathrm{\ppm}\) , where the limit was defined as the corresponding concentration of analyte with a spectrum-shift of \(1\,\textrm{n m}\) [37]. We also calculated the detection limits for other chemicals (TOL, DCM, ACE, TCM, MeOH, XTL, IPA, and Hex) to be 9.1, 10.0, 16.7, 8.3, 11.1, 13.5, 20, and \(25\;\mathrm{ppm}\) (Fig. 3(d) and Figs. S8 and S9 in the ESM). Figure 3(e) displays response time of ethanol sensing on the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO2 PCs, which was calculated from the time-dependent reflectance recorded for every 0.5 s interval (Fig. 3(e), insert). The reflection signals of the PCs kept flat in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flow, which tended to a marked decrease and then fluctuated around the same level in ethanol vapor flow. Response time and recovery time of the platform were defined as the differential absorption when the signal reached \(95\%\) of the maximum absorption [49]. The response time of the PCs was 1.7, 3.9, 5.4, 7.4, and 9.8 s by exposure to \(100{-}500\;\mathrm{ppm}\) ethanol vapors (Fig. S10 in the ESM). Figure 3(f) shows the respective isotherms derived from the pressure dependent \(\Delta\lambda\) responses of the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO2 PCs to ethanol vapor of various
concentrations. All of them display similarly the steep increase in ethanol uptake at a low relative pressure \((<15\;\mathrm{Pa})\) and the saturation at \(15{-}25\;\mathrm{Pa}\), which indicated that ambient pressure has no effect on the adsorption process of the PCs. Thus, the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_2\) PCs exhibit fast response time and little response delay for chemical sensing, which exceeds the response of the reported MOFs-based sensor (Table S4 in the ESM).
To determine the generality of isostructural MOFs-based PCs that regulated by different types of nanomaterials, we used colloidal \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) NPs to regulate the refractive index of the HKUST-1 films and then to construct the multilayered structures by the sequential spraying coating method (Fig. 4). The \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) NPs with the size of \(\sim\,50\;\mathrm{nm}\) were synthesized by the hydrothermal method (Fig. S11 in the ESM), which had high dielectric constant and non-absorption in visible light [50, 51]. Also, the thickness and RI of the \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) NPs-encapsulated HKUST-1 films were tuned by changing the number of spraying cycles (Fig. S12 in the ESM). Figure 4(a) shows the tunable reflectance of the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@ZrO\(_2\) PCs by varying the period layer thicknesses and refractive index, which is designed by the Bragg's law (Eq. (1) and Table S5 in the ESM). Main reflection band of the PCs appeared at \(\lambda=440\), 543, and \(634\;\mathrm{nm}\) with the reflected color of blue, green, and red, which comprised 3 bilayers of HKUST-1/HKUST-1@ZrO\(_2\) systems with thickness of \(70\;\mathrm{nm}/80\;\mathrm{nm}\), \(80\;\mathrm{nm}/110\;\mathrm{nm}\), and \(60\;\mathrm{nm}/155\;\mathrm{nm}\), respectively. Similarly, the reflection band of the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@ZnO PCs appeared at \(\lambda=407\), 520, and \(604\;\mathrm{nm}\) with the reflected color of cyan, light-green, orange-red, which maintained bilayer thicknesses of \(70\;\mathrm{nm}/75\;\mathrm{nm}\), \(80\;\mathrm{nm}/100\;\mathrm{nm}\), and \(60\;\mathrm{nm}/150\;\mathrm{nm}\), respectively (Fig. 4(b) and Table S6 in the ESM). XRD pattern of isostructural MOFs-based PCs showed co-existence of the diffraction peaks of both the HKUST-1 and the \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) NPs, respectively (Fig. S13 in the ESM). Thus, the universal design
To determine how the weighted effect of different NPs supports specific sensing of ethanol on the isostructural MOFs-based PCs, we characterized their reflection band shift depending on the change of vapor concentrations (Fig. S1(a) and Fig. S15 in the ESM). The main reflection band of the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@ZnO PCs was around \(500\;\mathrm{nm}\) and shifted to \(530\;\mathrm{nm}\) by increasing the ethanol concentration from 0 to \(500\;\mathrm{ppm}\), which showed no significant change \((\Delta\lambda=30)\) with a linear increase slope of 0.015. For the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@ZrO\(_2\) PCs, the wavelength at maximum reflection was \(540\;\mathrm{nm}\) and changed to \(605\;\mathrm{nm}\) \((\Delta\lambda=65)\) with ethanol concentration at \(500\;\mathrm{ppm}\), which showed a linear increase with a slope of 0.16. Most importantly, the reflection band of the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_2\) PCs was at around \(465\;\mathrm{nm}\) and reached \(536\;\mathrm{nm}\) with ethanol concentration at \(500\;\mathrm{ppm}\), which showed a linear increase with a slope of 0.18. We found the detection limits of the \(\mathrm{ZnO}\), \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) encapsulated HKUST-1-based PCs for ethanol sensing were 16.67, 16.69, and 6.67 ppm, respectively. The result indicates that the
HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_2\) PCs promote the highest sensitivity for the specific sensing of ethanol, which was consistent with the weighted effect of \(\mathrm{TiO_{2}}\) NPs on chemical sensing (Fig. 2(b)).
We further characterized the evolution of time-dependent maximum reflectance of the reflection band when ethanol vapors were exposed to the isostructural MOFs-based PCs with various different NPs encapsulations, independently (Fig. 5(b)). Reflectance response of the PCs \(\left(\Delta R_{\mathrm{max}}/R_{0}(\%)\right)\) is defined as the ratio of maximum reflectance change \(\left(\Delta R_{\mathrm{max}}\right)\) to baseline resistance \(\left(R_{0}\right)\). The response of HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_2\) PCs varied from to \(10.0\%{-}19.2\%\) upon exposure to 100, 200, 300, 400, and \(500\ \mathrm{ppm}\) of ethanol. The response of HKUST-1/HKUST-1@ZrO\(_2\) PCs and HKUST-1/HKUST-1@ZnO PCs varied from \(9.5\%\) to \(12.4\%\) and \(8.0\ \%\) to \(11.3\ \%\) upon exposure to \(100{-}500\ \mathrm{ppm}\) of ethanol, respectively. The results indicate that HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_2\) PCs have a greater reflectance response to ethanol, implying a higher contribution of \(\mathrm{TiO_{2}}\) NPs to ethanol sensing. Notably, the response of HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_2\) PC increases linearly with increasing ethanol concentration, while the response of both HKUST-1/HKUST-1@ZrO\(_2\) PCs and HKUST-1/HKUST-1@ZnO PCs firstly increases and then remains stable. Interestingly, the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO\(_2\) PCs have the strongest response for specific ethanol sensing. From our previous reports, we proved that chemical sensing on the MOFs-based
\(1\mathrm{a}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) PCs and HKUST-1/HKUST-1@ZnO PCs varied from \(9.5\%\) to \(12.4\%\) and \(8.0\ \%\) to \(11.3\ \%\) upon exposure to \(100-\) \(500\,\mathrm{ppm}\) of ethanol, respectively. The results indicate that HKUST1/HKUST-1@TiO2 PCs have a greater reflectance response to ethanol, implying a higher contribution of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NPs to ethanol sensing. Notably, the response of HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO2 PC increases linearly with increasing ethanol concentration, while the response of both HKUST-1/HKUST-1@ZrO2 PCs and HKUST1/HKUST-1@ZnO PCs firstly increases and then remains stable. Interestingly, the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@TiO2 PCs have the strongest response for specific ethanol sensing. From our previous reports, we proved that chemical sensing on the MOFs-based
devices is affected by the following two factors: (i) replacement of gas molecules inside the MOFs cavity by the analyte; (ii) additional adsorption of the analyte to the encapsulated nanomaterials in the MOFs [39]. To elucidate the surface chemistry of the NPs on the specific chemical sensing of the HKUST-1/HKUST-1@NPs PCs, we conducted XPS analysis of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) (Fig. 5(c) and Fig. S16 in the ESM). O 1s region of the NPs can be fitted with three main peaks at binding energies of 530.8, 532.3, and \(533.6\;\mathrm{eV}\), which are attributed to the lattice oxygen in NPs \(\mathrm{(TiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{ZnO}\), \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups, and \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}\) respectively [40–42]. XPS characterization of HKUST-1/HKUST-1@NPs PCs showed that the ratio of \(-\mathrm{OH}/\) metal elements on the surface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}(0.60)\) was greater than the \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}(0.50)\) and \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) NPs(0.20). The results suggest that the \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups on the surface of NPs can modify the adsorption of MOFs-based PCs to advance specific chemical sensing through hydrogen bonding interactions. Thus, we have expanded the versatility of MOFs-based PCs by constructing HKUST-1/HKUST-1@NPs PCs, which can be tailored for the sensing of chemicals.
## 3 Conclusion
In summary, we developed isostructural MOFs-based PCs by stacking Bragg bilayers of HKUST-1 and HKUST-1@NPs thin films with the same MOFs lattice through a layer-by-layer assembly method. The monolithic HKUST-1 was prepared as low RI layer by LPE method. High RI layer of the HKUST-1@NPs was achieved by sequentially spray coating nanomaterials \(\mathrm{(TiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) NPs, independently) in HKUST-1 to form HKUST-1@TiO2, HKUST-1@ZrO2, and HKUST-1@ZnO thin films, respectively. The isostructural MOFs-based PCs were constructure on a large scale by sequential spraying coating of the HKUST-1 and HKUST-1@NPs to form the desired number Bragg bilayers. Bandwidth of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@NPs PCs were tailored by the thickness and RI of HKUST-1 and HKUST1@NPs layers, which demonstrate wide-range color tunable reflection. Reflectivity of our isostructural MOFs-based PCs is about \(96\%\). In addition, we demonstrated high detection sensitivity for a variety of chemical sensing on the HKUST1/HKUST-1@NPs PCs. By characterizing the evolution of time-dependent reflectance of the PCs, dynamic characteristics of chemical sensing showed that the specific adsorption process of the isostructural HKUST-1/HKUST-1@NPs PCs could be tailored due to the weighted effect of NPs for different chemicals. XPS measurement revealed that the specific sensing was induced by hydrogen bonding interactions between \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups on the surface of NPs and chemicals. We believe nanomaterials tailored isostructural MOFs-based PCs provide critical insights on totalreflectance integrated photonics, which is useful for real-time chemical sensing, security labeling, and smart display
